{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1666 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ff2004c678268fbdcdde84a44526b300ee2119d981ec651571ae5e8412e385e7" :edb/db-before-t 1665 :edb/db-after-t 1666 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534978 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:57:24.882Z" 13194139534978 true] [17592186128781 1100 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$. The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0$.*" 13194139534978 false] [17592186128781 1100 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/9e6d498215d04524e5eec7343a1463a80182f3050eaf9ad5e4766cf7f5f50f14.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" 13194139534978 true] [17592186128781 1145 "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}$. So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad v(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad w(x,y,z) = yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} &= 2x & \\frac{∂u}{∂y}|= 0 & \\frac{∂u}{∂z}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x}|= y & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}|= x & \\frac{∂v}{∂z}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x}|= 0 & \\frac{∂w}{∂y}|= z & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}|= y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2x(xy - 0) \\\\\n&= 2x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}\n$$\n\nin terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2}y &= 2 \\cdot x \\cdot (xy) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{u} \\cdot v \\\\\n&= 2v\\sqrt{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= ((∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)))^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534978 false] [17592186128781 1145 "For a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/8a59f6bb056b614f0489a4fbfcba7c97ca6fed48d754863514a6e08db5767cf2.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186128782 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534978 false] [17592186128782 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/ff9551358ebda05664fb636ce1cb0864a67845a3d424ae7ce36f590d1d43173c.png)" 13194139534978 true] [17592186128782 1145 "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad y(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad z(u,v,w) = u^2+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= -3u^{2} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w}|= 1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 2v & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 2u & \\frac{∂y}{∂w}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u}|= 2u & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}|= 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}|= 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}-3u^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3u^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3u^{2}(0 - 0) + 1(2v - 4u^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2(v - 2u^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(v - 2u^{2}) &= 0 \\\\\nv - 2u^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2$." 13194139534978 false] [17592186128782 1145 "For a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/5fc66b6d9c6c58bf7daa3fc8636a761a3abf5c2fbbf466d4a2da63f13a8fab22.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186128783 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx = 2u-v,\\qquad\ny = u+3v+w,\\qquad\nz = u+2v-w.\n$$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186128783 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n$x = 2u-v,\\qquad y = u+3v+w,\\qquad z = u+2v-w$." 13194139534978 false] [17592186128783 1145 "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 3 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w}|= 1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}|= 2 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}|=-1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(-3 - 2) + (-1 - 1) \\\\\n&=-10 - 2 \\\\\n&=-12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534978 false] [17592186128783 1145 "For a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/5fc66b6d9c6c58bf7daa3fc8636a761a3abf5c2fbbf466d4a2da63f13a8fab22.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186129204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324301 13194139534978 true] [17592186129204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324308 13194139534978 true] [17592186129204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324315 13194139534978 true] [17592186129205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324322 13194139534978 true] [17592186129205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324329 13194139534978 true] [17592186129205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324336 13194139534978 true] [17592186129205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324343 13194139534978 true] [17592186129206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324273 13194139534978 true] [17592186129206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324280 13194139534978 true] [17592186129206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324287 13194139534978 true] [17592186129206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324294 13194139534978 true] [17592186129461 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126572 13194139534978 false] [17592186129461 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324350 13194139534978 true] [17592186129461 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534978 true] [17592186129461 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - w^{2} \\\\ we^{u} \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534978 false] [17592186129461 1100 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186129461 1145 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186129665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534978 true] [17592186129665 1100 "Consider the transformation $T: (u, v, w) → (x(u, v, w), y(u, v, w), z(u, v, w))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y, z) → (u(x, y, z), v(x, y, z), w(x, y, z))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} + z \\\\ y^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$.\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x \\ne 0$ and $y > 0$.*" 13194139534978 false] [17592186129665 1100 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" 13194139534978 true] [17592186129665 1145 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =(\\frac{1}{x})^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186129687 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534978 true] [17592186129687 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4v \\\\ 3u^{2} + 2\\cos (vw) \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534978 false] [17592186129687 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4v \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw) \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534978 true] [17592186129687 1145 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}